696547a2fadd6ac85&v=0","mimeType":"image/svg+xml","sizes":["any"]}],"description":"Open the Adobe creative hub.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{}},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"destructiveHint":false,"openWorldHint":false},"_meta":{"ui":{"visibility":["app"],"resourceUri":"ui://adobe-home.html"},"openai/ui":{"entrypoints":[{"type":"global"}]},"resource_name":"adobe.adobe_home","_codex_apps":{"resource_uri":"/connector_69312da8e4dc81919370cb86fd172b6c/link_6abb1a24e0d88191bee88b0fb41f697e/adobe_home","contains_mcp_source":true},"connector_id":"connector_69312da8e4dc81919370cb86fd172b6c","connector_name":"Adobe","connector_description":"Design, combine, and edit","link_id":"link_6abb1a24e0d88191bee88b0fb41f697e","link_owner_profile":{}},"outputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"cards":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"kind":{"type":"string","enum":["inspiration","micro","app"]},"title":{"type":"string"},"subtitle":{"type":"string"},"badge":{"type":"string"},"accent":{"type":"string"},"image":{"type":"string"},"prompt":{"type":"string"},"tool":{"type":"string"},"appId":{"type":"string"},"url":{"type":"string"}},"required":["id","kind","title","subtitle","badge","accent","image"]}},"version":{"type":"string"},"logo":{"type":"string"}},"required":["cards"]}},{"name":"adobe.font_details","title":"Font Details","description":"Look up authoritative Adobe Fonts metadata for one or more fonts — text only,\nno image; this does not show what a font looks like — if a font-preview tool\nis available on this surface, use it instead for a visual specimen (its own\ndocstring links back here for the reverse case).\n\nArgs:\n    font_identifiers: Fonts to look up. Each requires postscript_name; font_id is\n        optional. The lookup has no combined-key mode — it resolves by exactly\n        one identifier per request — so when both are given, font_id is tried\n        first (it's the more precise identifier when valid), and postscript_name\n        is tried as a fallback only if that specific font_id lookup fails. Either\n        one resolving is enough; they are not cross-validated against each other,\n        so if both are given and disagree, the result silently reflects whichever\n        one succeeded — check the returned resolved_via if you need to know which.\n        A malformed postscript_name is only rejected as invalid_input\n        when font_id is absent; when font_id is present, a malformed\n        postscript_name is simply not attempted as a fallback candidate — no\n        error is raised about it specifically, so a failure in that case doesn't\n        distinguish \"font_id failed, postscript_name was fine but didn't match\"\n        from \"font_id failed, postscript_name was malformed and never tried.\"\n        postscript_name must match exactly, including case — this lookup does not\n        fuzzy-match or case-normalize (e.g. \"myriadpro-regular\" will not resolve\n        \"MyriadPro-Regular\"). Use an exact value, such as one already returned by\n        the font-recommendation tool, rather than guessing from a display name.\n    locale: Language the returned font family/style names (and\n        recommendation/search group names) are translated into, when a\n        translation is available (default en_US). Hyphen,\n        bare-code, and region-mismatched forms are all\n        normalized to the closest supported locale (e.g. ja-JP\n        and ja both become ja_JP; en_GB becomes en_US); an\n        unsupported value falls back to en_US rather than being\n        rejected. See this\n        server's MCP instructions for the list of supported locales.\n\nReturns:\n    JSON string with a `results` list, one entry per requested font, in the same\n    order as the input. Each entry is either resolved metadata (family_name,\n    style_name, full_name, designers, foundry, detail_url, preview_url,\n    language_support, library_availability, weight, style, is_variable_font,\n    axes, resolved_via, requested, and a font_identifier or bare postscript_name)\n    or {\"postscript_name\": ..., \"error_code\": \"<code>\", \"user_message\": \"...\",\n    \"requested\": {...}} where error_code is one of invalid_input, not_found,\n    upstream_error, upstream_timeout, and user_message is human-readable text\n    safe to show the caller as-is.\n    weight is the numeric OpenType weight class (e.g. 400, 700); the same\n    class can look visually different across different families. style is\n    \"normal\" or \"italic\". Use these rather than inferring boldness or\n    slant from style_name or postscript_name text.\n    is_variable_font is always present (true/false). axes is present exactly\n    when is_variable_font is true (and only then) -- always a list, though it\n    may be empty ([]) for a variable font that exposes no usable named\n    instances -- a list of axis tags (e.g. [\"wght\", \"wdth\"])\n    this font exposes — tags only, deliberately no min/max/default. This tool has\n    no access to the font's true axis range, so it doesn't claim numeric ranges\n    it can't verify — only that these axes exist.\n    Do not guess at or state a specific min/max/default for an axis from this\n    tool's output.\n    resolved_via is \"font_id\" or \"postscript_name\" — whichever identifier\n    actually produced this result. When only one was supplied it's that one;\n    when both were supplied it tells you which one decided the outcome, since\n    they aren't cross-validated against each other. requested ({postscript_name,\n    font_id — font_id present only if you supplied one}) is always present, on\n    every entry, success or error, echoing exactly what you sent for that entry\n    (not the resolved identity) — use it to match a result back to its request\n    after filtering, reordering, or merging results downstream, rather than\n    relying on array position or re-deriving it from the resolved fields, which\n    only coincidentally match what was requested and don't when font_id\n    resolved to a different font than the postscript_name you sent.\n    font_identifier (rather than a bare postscript_name) appears whenever a\n    font_id could be resolved for that font — including when the input only\n    supplied postscript_name — so a bare-postscript_name lookup doubles as a\n    postscript_name-to-font_id resolver.\n    invalid_input includes a client-side format check against the standard\n    PostScript name character set (letters, digits, \".\", \"_\", \"-\", 1-255 chars)\n    and never reaches the network — retrying with the same value won't help.\n    not_found means the format was fine but no font matched it exactly.\n    language_support lists language codes (BCP 47/IANA subtags, e.g. \"ja\",\n    \"zh-Latn-pinyin\") for which this font's glyph coverage satisfies Adobe's\n    curated minimum-codepoint-set definition of that language (deliberately\n    excluding common punctuation) — not raw font cmap coverage or a marketing\n    claim. A missing code means the font doesn't meet that minimum, not that\n    Adobe Fonts lacks support for the language generally.\n    upstream_error means the lookup itself failed with a hard error.\n    upstream_timeout means the lookup hit a transient failure — a timeout,\n    a connection error, or a 5xx — so retrying may succeed.\n\nIf the user wants to see the font rather than only read about it, and a\nfont-preview tool is available on this surface, pass the returned\nfont_identifier as the one entry in a font_identifiers list to it to\nshow an inline specimen — this tool only returns metadata text, never\na rendered preview. If the\nuser wants other weights, widths, or italics in the same family instead\nof (or in addition to) this exact font, and a font-styles tool is\navailable on this surface, use it instead — this tool only returns the\nsingle font(s) requested, never sibling styles.\n","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"font_identifiers":{"items":{"$ref":"#/$defs/FontIdentifier"},"title":"Font Identifiers","type":"array"},"locale":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"default":"en_US","title":"Locale"}},"required":["font_identifiers"],"$defs":{"FontIdentifier":{"description":"A font's identity: mandatory postscript_name, optional font_id. Shared\nacross tools that consume this differently -- font_id is tried first in\nget_font_details/get_font_styles/get_font_preview, used to skip a\nresolution hop in add_fonts_to_cc_selections, and accepted but never\nconsumed in create_font_kit or get_similar_fonts (both look up by\npostscript_name alone) -- see each tool's own docstring for which\napplies.","properties":{"postscript_name":{"title":"Postscript Name","type":"string"},"font_id":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"default":null,"title":"Font Id"}},"required":["postscript_name"],"title":"FontIdentifier","type":"object"}},"title":"get_font_detailsArguments"},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"destructiveHint":false,"openWorldHint":false},"_meta":{"resource_name":"adobe.font_details","_codex_apps":{"resource_uri":"/connector_69312da8e4dc81919370cb86fd172b6c/link_6abb1a24e0d88191bee88b0fb41f697e/font_details","contains_mcp_source":true},"connector_id":"connector_69312da8e4dc81919370cb86fd172b6c","connector_name":"Adobe","connector_description":"Design, combine, and edit","link_id":"link_6abb1a24e0d88191bee88b0fb41f697e","link_owner_profile":{}},"outputSchema":{"properties":{"result":{"title":"Result","type":"string"}},"required":["result"],"title":"get_font_detailsOutput","type":"object"}},{"name":"adobe.interactive_image_remove_background_app","title":"Remove background","description":"**Session handle:** each source image (`originalUploadedImage`) has its own `sessionId`, minted on demand — it is not one handle for the whole conversation. The **first** call for a given source image must pass `sessionId: -1`; wait for the `sessionId` it returns and reuse that value for every later call on that same image. When `originalUploadedImage` changes to a different image, pass `-1` again to start that image's session, and keep a per-image mapping so returning to an earlier image reuses its `sessionId`.\n\nRemove background from your image and export with a transparent background in PNG format.\n\n**ROUTING — this widget owns every single-image request for background removal to a transparent cutout.** Count only the images this turn asks you to edit, not what the session holds.\n- **1 image this turn:** call THIS tool. Never ask widget-vs-server-side; never offer the direct tools (`image_remove_background`).\n- **2+ images this turn:** NEVER this tool — no batch form; use those direct tools and do not mention this one.\n- **1-image exceptions, use those direct tools when:** the user explicitly asks you to do it for them (\"just do it\", \"you pick\") — an explicit ask only, never inferred from terseness, a stated value, or impatience; a solid-color backdrop is wanted instead of transparency (`options.backgroundColor`).\n- **STOP after calling.** It renders its own result and returns no asset URL — no `asset_inline_preview`, `asset_preview_file`, `interactive_image_edit_preview`, or any next step.\n- **Non-generative** — never widens what generative editing is permitted.\n\n**Requires a mask:**\n- If no maskURI exists for this image, call image_select_subject_action_json tool first.\n- Verify the mask was generated from the same image being edited.\n- The mask required for this tool must be the background mask.\n   → If the mask is the subject mask, set toBeInverted to true.\n   → If the mask is the background mask, set toBeInverted to false.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"imageURI":{"type":"string","description":"The image to edit (accessible http/https URL). Choose by first match: (1) a new image uploaded with this request; (2) the previous tool's `outputUrl` if it just produced one; (3) the SAME `imageURI` you passed to the previous `*_app` edit — reuse it so consecutive effects stack on one base (a 2nd effect uses the 1st's input, NOT its result); (4) `originalUploadedImage`, the session's source image. NEVER use `currentInputImage` or any rendition — that is the flattened result and is for destructive tools only. Don't call `image_get_context` for this."},"originalUploadedImage":{"type":"string","description":"The source image this edit session is built on, and the key its `sessionId` is bound to. If an image was just uploaded to chat, use its reference; otherwise copy `originalUploadedImage` verbatim from the widget state of the image you are editing. It MUST stay constant while you keep editing the same image — NEVER set it to an edited, rendition, or tool-output image, which starts a new, empty history and loses prior edits. Each distinct `originalUploadedImage` has its own `sessionId`: whenever this changes to a different source image, pass `sessionId: -1` to start a new session for it."},"sessionId":{"type":"string","description":"The widget-state session handle, unique to each source image (`originalUploadedImage`) — not one handle for the whole conversation. Set it to the `sessionId` a previous image tool returned for the image you are editing now. If that image has no `sessionId` yet (a newly uploaded image, or you switched to a different one), set it to `-1`; a new handle is minted and returned — reuse that value for every later call on this image. Keep a `sessionId` per source image: returning to an earlier image reuses the handle from that image, not `-1`."},"mask":{"type":"object","properties":{"maskURI":{"type":"string","description":"The URI of the mask to be applied to the image. **Reusing a mask (only when the editing context with `actionHistory` is available):** the user can edit or remove masks in the editor, so a mask URL you passed earlier may be stale — the context's `actionHistory` lists each edit with its current mask. To carry a mask forward from an earlier edit, find that edit's most recent entry and pass its `mask.maskURI`, not the URL you originally passed; `isMaskUpdated` means the user edited it, so keep `maskDescription` / `toBeInverted` unchanged. If the entry has `userRemovedMaskURI` instead of `maskURI`, the user removed that mask: don't reuse it — call a selection tool if the request still targets that region, and pass `userRemovedMaskURI` only if the user explicitly asks for that mask again. To use the original AI selection instead of the user's edit, or after a crop, expand, straighten or undo-all, call a selection tool again. Use the attached context when present; call `interactive_image_get_context` only when none is attached."},"maskDescription":{"type":"string","description":"The description of what the mask is selecting."},"toBeInverted":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether the mask should be inverted, in order to select the inverted area."}},"required":["maskURI","maskDescription","toBeInverted"]},"replaceLastEdit":{"description":"Boolean value that tells the widget to undo the previous effect before applying the next requested.\n**For Example** a prompt like \"undo the last effect and apply a twirl\" would set this boolean to true to undo the last effect before applying what is requested in this prompt. With this input parameter the undoEdit tool does not need to be called before calling the next effect tool","type":"boolean"}},"required":["imageURI","originalUploadedImage","sessionId","mask"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":false,"destructiveHint":false,"idempotentHint":false,"openWorldHint":false},"_meta":{"openai/outputTemplate":"ui://widget/pure-editor-carousel.html","openai/toolInvocation/invoking":"Removing background","openai/toolInvocation/invoked":"Background removed","ui":{"resourceUri":"ui://widget/pure-editor-carousel.html"},"resource_name":"adobe.interactive_image_remove_background_app","_codex_apps":{"resource_uri":"/connector_69312da8e4dc81919370cb86fd